#
#                    THIS FILE IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE.
#
# It is empty because the integration boundary has NEVER been analysed, and you
# cannot waive findings you have not seen. Everything below is an explanation of
# what must not be added to it and why — not a to-do list.
#
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. WHY THE BLOCK-LEVEL WAIVERS WERE NOT PORTED WHOLESALE
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nine blocks below this level ship waiver files. Copying them in would produce
# a first run that looks clean, and a blanket waiver is precisely how a real
# crossing hides. Three concrete reasons, each verified rather than assumed:
#
# (a) The vendor-IP justification does not hold for this build.
#     tidelink/cdc/waiver.swl:43-45 and :51 waive `-du "Wlink"`,
#     `-du "Wav*" -regexp`, `-du "Wlink*" -regexp` and `-du "wlink_*" -regexp`,
#     justified in-file as covering untouched Chisel-generated vendor IP. That
#     justification is FALSE here:
#     tidelink/src/rtl/local_overrides/ contains 33 locally-modified module
#     copies, and 24 of them match those patterns. Among the waived-but-modified:
#         wlink_wlink_ptp_tl_a2l_48x4.v      the a2l mailbox itself
#         WlinkGenericFCReplayV2_12.v / _13.v carry the a2l CDC fix that was
#                                             never ported to the AW/W/B siblings
#                                             — which those patterns ALSO waive
#         WavMultibitSync_18.v               a locally-modified SYNCHRONISER
#                                             PRIMITIVE, waived as a "verified
#                                             CDC cell"
#     So the blanket patterns suppress locally-modified logic sitting on the
#     exact path with a known open bug. Nothing matching Wlink*/Wav*/wlink_* is
#     carried forward. If vendor IP has to be scoped out later, waive by
#     EXPLICIT MODULE NAME and list in the comment which of those names are
#     local overrides and therefore not vendor IP at all.
#
# (b) The legitimate synchronisers should come back as RESULTS, not silence.
#     This design contains real, correct 2-flop synchronisers, and the value of
#     a first CDC run is that the tool RECOGNISES them and says so. Waiving them
#     up front throws away the only evidence that they are there and connected:
#         WavMultibitSync / WavDemetReset* / WavDemetSet
#           tidelink/deps/axi-chiplet-controller/logical/wlink/
#           (WavMultibitSync.v, WavDemetSet.v, WavDemetReset_107/_124/_129.v)
#           — mostly inside the black-boxed controller for now; they become
#             visible when B8 removes that stop_module.
#         OpenCores MAC wishbone syncs
#           .../ethernet-mac-ahb/src/rtl/ethmac_patches/eth_wishbone.v:458-460
#           (TxRetrySync1, TxAbortSync1, TxDoneSync1), :770-788
#           (BlockingTxStatusWrite_sync1/_sync2), :1215-1254 (TxStartFrm_sync1)
#         eth_rx_cksum MII<->fabric chains
#           .../ethernet-mac-ahb/src/rtl/eth_rx_cksum.v:409-417
#           (rptr_gray_mrx_s0/_s1, mode_mrx_s0/_s1) and :482-491, :512-515
#           (ovf_tog_pclk_s0/_s1/_s2, push_tog_pclk_s0/_s1/_s2)
#         CM0+ DAP CDC cells
#           cm0p_dap_ap_cdc.v, cm0p_dap_dp_cdc.v, cm0p_dap_cdc_capt_sync.v
#           — deliberately NOT black-boxed (see cdc/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.prj)
#     Expect these as Ac_sync* / informational lines. If any of them shows up as
#     UNSYNCHRONISED instead, that is a finding, not a waiver candidate.
#
# (c) Block waivers are scoped to block tops that are not what this chip builds.
#     nanosoc-multicore-system/ethernet-subsystem-ahb/cdc/ethernet_ss_ahb.sgdc:19
#     says `current_design ethernet_ss_ahb`, but the SoC instantiates
#     ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii (nanosoc_multicore_soc.sv:834). Its waivers apply to a
#     top this chip does not contain.
#
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. checkSGDC_05 MUST STAY ACTIVE — DO NOT WAIVE IT HERE
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tidelink/cdc/waiver.swl:133-134 carries a GLOBAL, UNSCOPED waiver:
#
#     waive -rule checkSGDC_05    \
#           -comment "abstract_port auto-migrated for stop_module'd subblocks"
#
# checkSGDC_05 is the UNMATCHED-abstract_port detector. It is the tree's only
# automatic guard against the exact failure this whole setup is written to
# avoid: a port gets renamed, the SGDC name no longer matches, SpyGlass drops
# the constraint, DEFAULTS the port to some other clock, and reports crossings
# that do not exist — or worse, stops reporting ones that do.
#
# That waiver switches off the alarm for the failure mode. It is not carried
# forward, and nothing in this file may waive checkSGDC_05, SGDC_abstract_port02
# or checkSGDC_01. If one of them fires, a port name in
# cdc/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sgdc has drifted from the RTL and the fix is to
# correct the name, not to silence the check.
#
# The port lists in the SGDC were validated against the RTL when written (all
# 111 chiplet ports, and all axi_chiplet_controller ports, matched in both
# directions). These rules are what keeps that true after the next RTL change.
#
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. HOW TO ADD A WAIVER HERE, IF ONE IS EVER JUSTIFIED
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Never `-du` a module that has a local override in
#     tidelink/src/rtl/local_overrides/ or src/rtl/local_overrides/ — it is not
#     vendor IP, whatever its name looks like.
#   * Never waive a whole rule globally. Scope it: -du <exact name>, or
#     -file, or -from/-to.
#   * Prefer fixing the CONSTRAINT over waiving the finding. Most first-run
#     noise at an integration boundary is a missing abstract_port, not a real
#     crossing — and a missing constraint over-reports, which is safe. A waiver
#     is the opposite: it removes evidence.
#   * State what makes the crossing safe, with file:line. "Pre-verified IP" is
#     not a justification; it is a claim, and (a) above is what happens when
#     nobody rechecks it.
#
# (No waivers. Intentionally.)
